// ---------- Long-form guide (paragraph strings may contain inline HTML) ----------

const guide = [
  {
    id: "who-we-are",
    heading: "Who we are and how remote delivery works for Canadian clients",
    paragraphs: [
      `BtechWaleTech is three engineers in India. Ankur Kumar is a full stack developer. Santosh Sharma covers AI and machine learning, AWS, data science, technical SEO and deployment. Vedansh Shrivastava runs project management along with data science, AI and automation. That is the whole team, and the person who answers your email is one of the people who will write your code. There is no sales team and no hand-off to a delivery group you never meet.`,
      `We have no Canadian office, no business number and no address in Toronto, Montreal or Vancouver. We would rather say that plainly than hide behind a forwarding address, and it matters for practical reasons too: a .ca domain, a Moneris merchant account and a Google Business Profile all have to be in your name, not ours, which is exactly how we set them up. Read more <a href="/about/">about the team</a> before you decide anything.`,
      `<strong>How a remote project runs.</strong> It starts with a call in your morning, which is our evening, and a short written questionnaire covering goals, audience, provinces served, languages, pages, integrations and deadlines. You receive a written scope and an itemized quote. Once you approve it, a private staging link goes up early in the build and stays live until launch, so you and your colleagues review real pages on your own devices. Written updates arrive on a fixed schedule, weekly on small projects and twice weekly on larger ones, covering what shipped, what is next, anything blocked and anything we need from you.`,
      `<strong>The time gap works in your favour.</strong> Canadian clients tend to send feedback in the afternoon. We work through it during our day, which is your night, and the changes are usually on staging before you are back at your desk. That cycle is especially useful on bilingual projects, where English and French versions of the same page often go through separate review rounds with different people.`,
      `<strong>Tools and access.</strong> We work over email, WhatsApp, Slack, Google Meet, Zoom or Microsoft Teams, in English. We can work inside your GitHub or GitLab organization and your existing cloud accounts, and if your policies limit who can see personal data in production, we plan our access around that from the start rather than after launch.`,
      `<strong>When we are the wrong fit.</strong> If you need someone on site several times a month, a Canadian entity to satisfy a procurement rule, French copywriting from the vendor itself, or a large team on call around the clock, a local agency is the better choice, and we will tell you so on the first call.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "services-detail",
    heading: "Services for the Canadian market, in detail",
    paragraphs: [
      `<strong>Web development.</strong> Most Canadian business sites we build are static or statically generated pages on a CDN, which keeps them fast on office fibre in downtown Toronto and on a patchy mobile signal on a highway outside Thunder Bay. A static site of up to 100 pages starts from ${P.site}, with custom design, mobile layouts, contact and quote forms, on-page SEO, structured data, hosting and deployment. Larger content sites built for search, with 700 or more pages generated from structured data such as services and towns, start from ${P.seoSite}. If your team wants to edit pages without us, we connect a headless CMS or build a lean WordPress site. The <a href="/services/web-development/">web development service page</a> has more on the stack.`,
      `<strong>Website design.</strong> Good design for Canadian buyers is mostly about removing doubt. We show service areas plainly, format phone numbers and postal codes the way people expect, use Canadian spelling, and show prices in CAD with the tax wording your accountant approves. For bilingual sites, both languages are part of the design from the first mock-up, so French headings, buttons and navigation have room instead of being squeezed into an English-sized box. Accessibility is designed in, not bolted on: readable type, strong contrast, clear focus states and forms that work with a keyboard and a screen reader.`,
      `<strong>Ecommerce.</strong> Stores on Shopify, WooCommerce or a custom stack, starting from ${P.shop}, with Stripe, Shopify Payments, PayPal, Square or Moneris, provincial sales tax rules and Canadian shipping. The ecommerce section below goes into detail.`,
      `<strong>SEO for Google in Canada.</strong> Technical SEO, on-page work, content and local search, with en-CA and fr-CA hreflang done properly and, where you also sell into the US, en-US pages that do not cannibalize your Canadian ones. Monthly SEO starts from ${P.seo}; see the <a href="/services/seo-services/">SEO services page</a> and the SEO section below.`,
      `<strong>Custom software and web apps.</strong> Custom web applications start from ${P.app}. Canadian projects we are suited to include booking and scheduling for clinics and trades, client portals for accounting and consulting firms, estimating tools for contractors, inventory dashboards for small manufacturers, volunteer and donor tools for nonprofits, and admin panels that replace a tangle of spreadsheets. We build the first useful version, put it in front of real users and extend it from there, hosted in a Canadian cloud region if you prefer.`,
      `<strong>AI automation.</strong> AI automation starts from ${P.ai} and fits repetitive work: routing enquiries, extracting data from invoices and shipping documents, drafting product descriptions, or answering routine questions from your own documentation. For bilingual businesses, automation can detect whether an enquiry arrived in English or French, route it to the right person and draft a first reply in the same language for a human to check before it goes out. We keep a person in the loop for anything customer-facing and do not send personal information to third-party AI services unless you have approved it, which matters under Quebec's Law 25 in particular.`,
      `<strong>Maintenance.</strong> The first five months after launch are included free: content updates in both languages, bug fixes, security updates, backups, uptime and speed checks, and basic SEO health checks. Once those months end, you can keep a plan from ${P.care} or simply message us when a change comes up. The <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> lists what each plan covers.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "bilingual",
    heading: "Bilingual English and French websites, built properly",
    paragraphs: [
      `If you sell to customers in Quebec, or to French speakers in New Brunswick, eastern and northern Ontario or Manitoba, French is not an afterthought. Quebec's language rules generally expect businesses operating there to make commercial content, including websites, available in French, and French speakers anywhere in the country trust a site more when it is written for them rather than machine-translated. Ask your adviser how the rules apply to your business; our job is to make sure the site can meet them without a rebuild.`,
      `<strong>Structure.</strong> Each language lives on its own URLs, such as /en/ and /fr/, or on separate subdomains if you prefer. Each page carries en-CA and fr-CA hreflang annotations pointing at its equivalent, plus an x-default. Titles, meta descriptions, structured data, image alt text, sitemaps and even URL slugs exist in both languages, so a French searcher sees a French result with a French URL. The language switcher takes visitors to the equivalent page, not back to the home page, and the site remembers their choice.`,
      `<strong>Design and testing.</strong> French text typically runs noticeably longer than English, which breaks cramped buttons, menus and card layouts designed only for English. We design with French strings from the first mock-up and test every template in both languages on narrow phone screens. Forms, validation and error messages, confirmation pages, transactional emails, PDF invoices, cookie banners and the privacy page all exist in both languages, because a French page with an English error message undermines the whole effort.`,
      `<strong>Who writes the French.</strong> Not us. Our team does not write professional French copy, and we are upfront about that. You supply it, or a translator you trust does, and we provide a simple handover format so both language versions stay in sync. Canadian French also differs from European French in vocabulary and tone, so a Quebec-based translator is usually worth the cost.`,
      `<strong>Starting in English only.</strong> If you only need English now, we structure the site so French can be added later without a rebuild. Retrofitting a second language into a site never designed for one costs far more than planning for it from the start.`,
      `<strong>Other languages.</strong> Some businesses in the Greater Toronto Area and the Lower Mainland also serve large Punjabi-, Chinese-, Tagalog-, Arabic- or Spanish-speaking communities. We build the same multilingual structure for any additional language, with the text supplied and checked by fluent speakers.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "ecommerce-ca",
    heading: "Ecommerce in Canada: Moneris, Stripe, Shopify, sales taxes and shipping",
    paragraphs: [
      `Ecommerce builds start from ${P.shop}. For Canadian stores, three details decide whether checkout actually works: sales taxes by province, prices in Canadian dollars, and shipping to a very large country with expensive rural and northern delivery.`,
      `<strong>Sales taxes.</strong> Tax depends on where the customer is. Alberta and the three territories charge GST only. Ontario, New Brunswick, Nova Scotia, Prince Edward Island and Newfoundland and Labrador use a single harmonized sales tax (HST). British Columbia, Saskatchewan and Manitoba charge GST plus a provincial sales tax, and Quebec charges GST plus QST. Rates change from time to time, some products are treated differently, and registration thresholds apply, so your accountant should confirm your registrations and product treatment. We configure the store to apply those rules by shipping address and show taxes clearly on the cart, receipt, invoice and confirmation email, with your registration numbers where your accountant says they should appear.`,
      `<strong>Payment providers.</strong> Shopify, which started in Ottawa, is the default for most product brands, with Shopify Payments handling cards and wallets. Stripe suits subscriptions, B2B invoicing, marketplaces and checkouts embedded in web apps. Square suits businesses that also sell in person. Moneris, the large Canadian processor owned by two of the big banks, and Helcim are good choices when you want a Canadian processor or your bank already offers one. PayPal works well as a second option. We integrate whichever you choose, test refunds, failed cards and partial orders, and keep card details with the processor, never on your server.`,
      `<strong>Shipping.</strong> Live rates from Canada Post, Purolator, UPS, FedEx or a shipping app such as ShipStation, flat-rate zones, free-shipping thresholds, local pickup and delivery for bricks-and-mortar shops, and sensible rules for rural, remote and northern postal codes where costs jump. For cross-border orders to the US, we can show duties and taxes clearly or offer delivered-duty-paid options through your shipping provider.`,
      `<strong>Bilingual stores.</strong> Stores selling in Quebec need French product names, descriptions, checkout, emails and policies, not just a translated home page. Shopify Markets and multilingual WooCommerce setups both handle this when configured properly.`,
      `<strong>Marketing plumbing.</strong> Google Merchant Center feeds with CAD prices, Meta catalogue sync, Klaviyo or Mailchimp flows for abandoned carts and post-purchase emails, and product structured data for price and availability. Marketing sign-ups at checkout use a separate, unticked box, in line with CASL.`,
    ],
