  {
    id: "seo-ca",
    heading: "SEO for Google in Canada: local, national and bilingual",
    paragraphs: [
      `Canadian search results are local and often split by language. Someone in Mississauga searching for an accountant sees different results from someone in Laval, and a French query in Montreal pulls a different set again. Every site we build ships with the groundwork: clean URLs, unique titles and descriptions written for Canadian queries, business and service structured data, XML sitemaps per language, fast mobile pages and internal links that help crawlers understand what matters.`,
      `<strong>Local SEO.</strong> For businesses with a location or service area, we help you set up or tidy your Google Business Profile, owned and verified by you, with the right primary category, service areas, hours, services and real photographs. We keep your business name, address and phone number identical across the listings that already mention you, such as Apple Business Connect, Bing Places, Yelp, Yellow Pages Canada and industry directories. We then build city and service pages with genuinely different content for each area you serve: the neighbourhoods covered, typical jobs, travel charges or response times. Spelling follows Canadian usage, prices show in CAD and phone numbers are formatted the way your customers dial them.`,
      `<strong>French SEO.</strong> French keyword research is done in French, not translated from English, because people search differently in each language and Quebec French has its own vocabulary. A Montreal renovation company, for example, needs pages built around the words Quebecers actually use, which a literal translation of English keywords often misses.`,
      `<strong>Canada and the US.</strong> Many Canadian businesses also sell into the United States, and the two markets can end up competing in search. Where prices, shipping or regulations differ, we build separate pages for each market with en-CA, fr-CA and en-US hreflang and an x-default, and make canonicals, sitemaps and internal links agree, which is where most multi-region setups quietly break. Where the versions would be nearly identical, we usually recommend one version that mentions both markets. Our <a href="/usa/">USA page</a> covers the American side.`,
      `<strong>AI search.</strong> AI Overviews and answer engines increasingly summarize local and service results. Clear, factual pages that answer specific questions, visible business details, structured data and crawlable HTML help there as much as in classic search, and we build with that in mind in both languages.`,
      `<strong>Monthly SEO and reporting.</strong> Monthly SEO starts from ${P.seo} and covers technical fixes, new content on a schedule, internal linking, listing work and a monthly report built on Search Console data. For bilingual sites, content can be planned for both languages, with the French supplied or approved by your translator. Expect movement over three to six months, and expect honest reports, including months where rankings stood still.`,
