  {
    id: "compliance",
    heading: "Compliance: PIPEDA, Quebec Law 25, CASL, AODA, French language rules and GST/HST",
    paragraphs: [
      `This section describes how we build, not legal advice. We are engineers, not lawyers. We do not certify compliance with any law, and we recommend a qualified adviser reviews your privacy policy, consent wording and anything that depends on how a law applies to your organization. What we do is build the site so that review has very little to fix.`,
      `<strong>PIPEDA.</strong> The Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act governs how private-sector organizations handle personal information in commercial activities across Canada, with Quebec, Alberta and British Columbia running their own substantially similar private-sector laws for activity within those provinces. It expects meaningful consent, collection limited to what you need, safeguards, a way for people to access their information, and reporting of breaches that pose a real risk of significant harm. For a website, that becomes concrete build decisions: forms that ask only for fields you use, HTTPS everywhere, access-controlled storage instead of an open spreadsheet, and a privacy page describing what actually happens to the data. Federal privacy reform has been proposed and stalled before; your adviser can tell you whether anything newer affects you.`,
      `<strong>Quebec Law 25.</strong> Quebec's modernized privacy law, phased in between 2022 and 2024, goes further than most. Technologies that identify, locate or profile visitors are expected to be off by default until the person chooses otherwise, so we configure consent banners that genuinely block analytics, ad pixels and embedded trackers until consent is given, in French and English. Organizations need a person in charge of the protection of personal information, whose contact details are published, and a privacy policy written in clear terms. Law 25 also generally expects a privacy impact assessment before personal information is communicated outside Quebec, which is relevant when a supplier elsewhere, including us, can access it. We keep that access minimal and describe it in writing so your privacy officer can assess it.`,
      `<strong>CASL.</strong> Canada's Anti-Spam Legislation generally requires consent before sending commercial electronic messages, clear identification of the sender in each message and an unsubscribe mechanism that is honoured promptly. We build marketing sign-ups as a separate, unticked checkbox with clear wording, store when and how consent was given, and connect forms to email platforms that handle unsubscribes properly. A contact form enquiry is never silently added to a newsletter list.`,
      `<strong>AODA and accessibility.</strong> Ontario's Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act requires many organizations, including private and non-profit organizations with 50 or more employees in Ontario and the public sector, to make their websites meet WCAG 2.0 level AA, with some exceptions. Manitoba, Nova Scotia and British Columbia have their own accessibility laws with standards being phased in, and the federal Accessible Canada Act applies to federally regulated organizations. We build to WCAG 2.1 AA practices by default: semantic structure, keyboard navigation, visible focus, sufficient contrast, labelled forms, alt text and captions where you supply video, tested with a screen reader before launch. We do not install overlay widgets that promise instant compliance.`,
      `<strong>French language requirements.</strong> In Quebec, the Charter of the French Language, strengthened by Bill 96, generally expects commercial content, websites included, to be available in French, and the French version should not be less complete than any other. Federal institutions have obligations under the Official Languages Act, and New Brunswick is officially bilingual. Our part is building sites where French is a full, first-class version; which rules apply to you is a question for your lawyer.`,
      `<strong>GST/HST and provincial taxes.</strong> As covered in the ecommerce section, we configure checkout to apply the taxes your accountant confirms. How you treat our own invoices as a supplier outside Canada is also a question for your accountant.`,
